# 9 The grid, the fields and the stencils {#ch:grid}

## Octree cells

`grid/octree.h` (or `quadtree.h` in 2D) builds the mesh by
recursive subdivision of one cube of side $L_0$. A cell at *level*
$\ell$ has side
$$
\Delta= \frac{L_0}{2^\ell}.
$$
Level 0 is the root. With $L_0 = 1$, level 8 gives $\Delta= 1/256 \approx 0.0039$
and level 10 gives $\Delta\approx 0.001$.
Every cell at every level exists in the tree, but only the *leaf*
cells (cells with no children) hold the solution. Coarser parent cells are
used by the multigrid solver ([chapter 16](16_multigrid)) and for interpolation
when the mesh changes.

Two neighbouring leaves may differ by at most one level. This
*2:1 balance* keeps stencils simple: every face of a leaf touches either
one leaf of the same level, one leaf one level coarser, or two (four in 3D)
leaves one level finer (figure 9.1).

