## Where each variable lives

Basilisk has three kinds of fields (figure 9.2):

- A `scalar` has one value per cell, at the centre. $p$, $f$,
  $\rho$ and each component of $\mathbf{u}$ are scalars.

- A `vector` is $d$ cell-centred scalars, for example $\mathbf{u}$ and the
  centred pressure-gradient field $\mathbf{g}$.

- A `face vector` has one value per face, normal to it. $\mathbf{u}_f$,
  $\alpha$, $\mu$, $\mathbf{a}$ and the embedded face fractions $f_s$ are face
  vectors. `uf.x[]` is on the left face of the cell and `uf.x[1]`
  on the right face.

![Figure 9.2: Positions of the variables. Pressure, volume fraction and the
centred velocity are at cell centres (dots). The face velocity $\mathbf{u}_f$, the
specific volume $\alpha$, the viscosity $\mu$ and the acceleration $\mathbf{a}$ are
on faces (arrows), normal to the face. The face gradient
$(p_i - p_{i-1})/\Delta$ is naturally located at the face ${i-\frac 12}$, next to
$u_{f,{i-\frac 12}}$.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/a-k-sangwan/basilisk-guide/main/basilisk_guide/figures/fig_placement.png)

> **In the code: `common.h`**
>
> ~~~literatec
> #define face_gradient_x(a,i) ((a[i] - a[i-1])/Delta)
> #define face_value(a,i)      ((a[i] + a[i-1])/2.)
> #define center_gradient(a)   ((a[1] - a[-1])/(2.*Delta))
> ~~~
>
> The three basic stencils: gradient at a face, average to a face, centred
> gradient at a cell.

## Ghost cells

To apply the same stencil in every leaf, including next to the domain
boundary, Basilisk surrounds the domain with *ghost cells*. Before a
stencil reads a field, the ghost values are filled from the boundary
conditions ([chapter 18](18_boundary)). With embedded boundaries `BGHOSTS = 2`, so
two layers are filled, because the cut-cell interpolations use a $5\times5$
stencil.

Fine/coarse faces inside the tree are handled the same way: when a fine cell
needs a neighbour that only exists at the coarser level, the value is
*prolongated* (interpolated) from the coarse cell into a temporary
ghost child.

## Restriction and prolongation {#sec:restrict}

*Restriction* fills a parent from its $2^d$ children; the default is the
average,
$$
s_{\text{parent}} = \frac{1}{2^d}\sum_{\text{children}} s_c,
$$
which conserves the integral of $s$. *Prolongation* fills children from
parents. The default is bilinear interpolation; in 2D a child takes
$$
s_{\text{child}} = \tfrac{9}{16}s_0 + \tfrac{3}{16}(s_x + s_y) + \tfrac{1}{16}s_{xy},
$$
where $s_0$ is its parent and $s_x$, $s_y$, $s_{xy}$ the parent's neighbours
in the child's direction (figure 9.3). Some fields replace these
defaults: $f$ uses a geometric refinement that cuts the reconstructed
interface (`fraction_refine`), the face velocity uses a
divergence-preserving refinement (`refine_face_solenoidal`), and with
`conserving.h` the velocity uses a refinement that conserves momentum
([section 12.5](12_momentum#sec:momentum-refine)).

![Figure 9.3: Bilinear prolongation in 2D. The child (red) in the upper-right
quarter of its parent takes the weights shown in brackets, divided by 16.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/a-k-sangwan/basilisk-guide/main/basilisk_guide/figures/fig_prolong.png)

## Adaptive refinement with wavelets {#sec:wavelet}

`adapt_wavelet({fields}, {tolerances}, maxlevel)` decides where to
refine by asking how well each field can be predicted from the coarser
level. For each parent cell and each listed field $s$:

1.  restrict the children to the parent (average);

2.  prolongate the parent back to the children;

3.  the error estimate in each child is
    $e = |s_{\text{child}} - \text{prolongation}(\text{restriction}(s))|$.

If $e > \epsilon$ (the tolerance for that field) and the level is below
`maxlevel`, the cell is marked too coarse and refined. If
$e \le \epsilon/1.5$ for every field, its children may be merged. Between
the two thresholds nothing changes, which stops cells from flipping between
refined and coarse every step. The error $e$ is of the size of the second
derivative times $\Delta^2$, so refinement goes where the fields curve sharply:
at interfaces, near solid walls and in shear layers.

> **In the code: `grid/tree-common.h`, `adapt_wavelet()`**
>
> ~~~literatec
> double e = fabs(*b - s[]);       // *b: original child value, s[]: prolongated
> if (e > emax && level < maxlevel)       -> too_coarse (refine)
> else if (e <= emax/1.5 || level > maxlevel) -> too_fine (may coarsen)
> ~~~

## The metric fields $c_m$ and $f_m$

Every solver multiplies cell volumes by `cm[]` and face areas by
`fm.x[]`. On a plain Cartesian grid both are 1. With `embed.h` the
`metric` event sets `cm = cs` and `fm = fs`: the fraction of the
cell volume and of each face open to fluid. That single substitution is how
every operator in this document becomes a cut-cell operator around the
solid ([chapter 19](19_embed)). With `axi.h` the same fields carry the
radius for axisymmetric runs.

